The Selection-Inflation Framework v0.5: Measured Admission, Structural Completeness, and Write-Time Memory Governance for Autonomous Agents

Abstract

This revision demotes model-family labels from admission certificate to calibration prior and replaces the statistical completeness argument of prior versions with a structural one. The selection-inflation identity remains the central object: \phi_{\mathrm{sel}} = \Lambda_0 \phi_V, with \Lambda_0 the ratio of the verifier's endorsement rate among emitted false claims to its unconditional false-endorsement rate. We correct the audit detection bound to N \le \ln(1-\epsilon)/\ln q and show that it is operationally unusable for long horizons: at q=0.999 and \epsilon=0.05, the budget is 51 effects, not 2994. Completeness assurance must therefore be structural — deny-by-default mediation with no unmediated path — and q characterizes only the residual blind spot. We prove that untrusted-path taint persistence requires an all-strong-edge path, not merely one strong edge per path; the one-strong-edge condition is false by explicit counterexample. We replace the redundant \min\{\text{Kish},\text{eigenvalue}\} admission bound with the eigenvalue bound n_{\mathrm{eff}}^{\lambda} = K/\lambda_{\max}, which is always at most Kish, and require an item-bootstrap interval and a human-panel baseline. Memory governance adopts write-time origin binding, a modality dimension for self-poisoning, retrieval context as a derivation parent, and receiver-signed tool receipts. Evaluation redesigns Test B to compare same-family, cross-family, and human verifiers under a plausibility-stratified null with false-discovery control. Empirical claims are bounded to their measured datasets and conditions, and the reference identifiers are stated inline.

---

1. What changed and why

Three changes drive this version.

Family is a prior, not a certificate. The strongest empirical claim available is that family diversity is a weak and dataset-dependent predictor of error independence, not that it is uninformative. The nine-judge, seven-family panel of Kohli (arXiv:2605.29800) yields n_{\mathrm{eff}} = 2.18 (bootstrap 95% CI [2.07, 2.31]) and an eigenvalue estimate 2.16, with mean pairwise \phi = 0.391. But the same-family-versus-cross-family gap of approximately 0.047 is an MNLI result; on RewardBench the same-family correlation gap is larger, approximately 0.109, and a separate family audit reports a same-family lift of 3.4–8.4 percentage points with global effect size 0.067 (95% CI [0.053, 0.084]). The correct inference is not "family does not matter" but "family does not certify independence and its effect is not portable across claim distributions." Admission must therefore be measurement-based.

Statistical completeness is bounded far more tightly than previously stated. The prior version's budget N \le \ln \epsilon/\ln q was wrong by roughly a factor of sixty. The exact bound is N \le \ln(1-\epsilon)/\ln q. At \epsilon = 0.05 this gives N=5 for q=0.99, N=51 for q=0.999, and N=102 for q=0.9995. Per-event statistical observation cannot deliver completeness assurance at agent horizons. The guarantee must come from the mediation structure; q covers only what the structure leaves unobserved.

Taint persistence requires all-strong paths. The prior condition — every path from an external ancestor contains at least one edge of weight \ge \theta — is false. A path e_0 \xrightarrow{0.9} a \xrightarrow{0.1} m with \theta = 0.5 contains a strong edge and yet m does not inherit e_0's taint, because the weak edge a \to m contributes \bot and the join at m is \bot. The correct condition is that every path from e_0 to m is entirely composed of strong edges.

These corrections do not weaken the framework's posture. They move its guarantees from statistics that cannot hold at scale to structure that can be tested, and from thresholds that can be gamed to origin bindings that cannot.

