3. Structural completeness replaces statistical completeness

3.1 The corrected detection bound

Suppose the independent observation channel O observes each real effect with probability q, independently across effects, and the ledger omits an effect exactly when O fails to observe it. The probability that all N effects are observed is q^N, so the probability of at least one undetected omission is

P(\text{undetected}) = 1 - q^N.

Setting this below \epsilon and solving, using \ln q < 0,

1 - q^N \le \epsilon
\iff q^N \ge 1-\epsilon
\iff N \le \frac{\ln(1-\epsilon)}{\ln q}.

The prior version's N \le \ln \epsilon/\ln q is not a small-\epsilon approximation to this; it is a different and much more permissive expression. The numerical consequences for \epsilon = 0.05 are:

q N_{\max} (exact)
0.99 5
0.995 10
0.999 51
0.9995 102

Holding N = 3000 below 5\% would require q \ge 0.99998 on every effect. Per-event statistical observation therefore cannot carry completeness assurance at agent horizons. The bound also describes the probability that a blind spot exists, not the probability that an omission is detected; those are different events, and the latter is what completeness requires.

3.2 The structural guarantee

Completeness assurance must come from mediation structure, not from sampling. The deployment claim is:

S1 (mediation). Every effect-producing action reaches a mediated channel. There is no unmediated path from the agent process to a governed target.

S2 (bypass absence). The absence of unmediated paths is tested, not asserted: a bypass probe enumerates paths that reach a governed target without passing the gate, and a clean run is recorded as dated, version-pinned evidence.

A structural finding of this kind is true of the framework for every adopter and cannot be configured away; a clean probe run is evidence, not proof. The statistical q then characterizes only the residual: effects that traverse a mediated channel but are not captured by the observation channel. The system-level bound is

N \le \frac{\ln(1-\epsilon)}{\ln q_{\mathrm{sys}}},
\qquad
q_{\mathrm{sys}} := \min_{p \in \mathcal{P}} q_p,

where the minimum is over tool paths and the attacker is assumed to choose the weakest path. Proposition 4 remains valid: no system-level guarantee can exceed the guarantee on the path the attacker selects.

3.3 Forking, refusal semantics, and liveness

A Merkle consistency proof shows that a presented branch extends some previously accepted state; it does not show that the producer holds no other branch. Detecting forking requires a witness that retains the last checkpoint it accepted for a given producer-and-log identity and refuses a presented predecessor inconsistent with that retained state. Registration with a transparency service without per-log predecessor state proves inclusion, not non-equivocation.

Liveness assumption L1 (contractual). Within each interval of length T_c, at least one non-producer witness is online and responsive, retains its last accepted checkpoint outside the producer's administrative domain, and refuses inconsistent predecessors. The witness set is chosen so that no single administrative domain controls a majority. If L1 fails for longer than the damage window, forking is undetectable in principle and the residual bound does not apply to effects in that interval. T_c must satisfy T_c \le \texttt{damage\_window}.

3.4 Receiver-signed receipts and context-as-parent

The tool receipt must be signed by the receiver (the tool gateway), not only by the agent, and must bind to the same action identifier as the checkpoint. The ledger's observer_ref then records which independent channel also observed the effect, making the injective map of completeness checkable. Additionally, the retrieval context at generation time is a derivation parent, not merely the content the agent cites. If a memory entry m is produced while a poisoned context c is active, c belongs in \mathrm{anc}(m) even if the agent does not quote it. Omitting context-as-parent is a declassification channel of the same kind as an unrecorded paraphrase.

