4. Memory governance: all-strong paths and write-time origin

4.1 Taint lattice and the corrected persistence condition

Let \mathbb{T} = \{\bot, \mathsf{T}, \mathsf{U}, \mathsf{X}, \mathsf{P}\} with \bot < \mathsf{T} < \mathsf{U} < \mathsf{X} < \mathsf{P}, join \vee = \max. For edge weight w and threshold \theta,

w \otimes t :=
\begin{cases}
t, & w \ge \theta,\\
\bot, & w < \theta.
\end{cases}

This operator is monotone in t. The propagation rule at m is

t(m) = \bigvee_{e \in \mathrm{anc}(m)} \big(w(e \to m) \otimes t(e)\big).

Proposition (corrected untrusted-path persistence). If every path from an ancestor e_0 with t(e_0) \ge \mathsf{X} to m consists entirely of edges of weight \ge \theta, then t(m) \ge \mathsf{X}.

Proof. Consider the subgraph G_\theta containing exactly the edges of weight \ge \theta. The hypothesis says e_0 and m lie in the same connected component of G_\theta. Induct on the length of a path in G_\theta from e_0 to m. Base: length zero, m = e_0, t(m) = t(e_0) \ge \mathsf{X}. Step: let e be the penultimate node on a strong path, so e \to m has weight \ge \theta. By the induction hypothesis t(e) \ge \mathsf{X}. Then w(e \to m) \otimes t(e) = t(e) \ge \mathsf{X}, and the join at m is at least that value. Hence t(m) \ge \mathsf{X}. \square

The one-strong-edge condition is false by the counterexample e_0 \xrightarrow{0.9} a \xrightarrow{0.1} m with \theta = 0.5: the edge e_0 \to a is strong, but a \to m is weak and contributes \bot, so t(m) = \bot. The correct operational rule is to compute the strong-edge subgraph and to require that every external ancestor of a sensitive-action justification be in the same strong component as the justification.

4.2 Write-time origin binding

The derivation DAG is not a stable ground truth: lineage is malleable through summarization, trusted-tool echo, and manufactured corroboration. Taint must be assigned at first write by the ingesting component and immutably bound to the entry. Later derivations may not lower it; an edge that would carry a lower taint than its parent is rejected, not treated as a legitimate declassification. This makes propagation monotone by construction rather than by threshold tuning. Any memory that can justify a sensitive action must carry a write-time origin certificate that cannot be altered by subsequent transformation.

4.3 Modality and self-poisoning

The lattice governs origin, not modality. A speculation promoted to a factual claim should require new external evidence, not merely a recorded derivation edge. Add a second dimension, or gate promotion on an external evidence reference. The empirical basis is that self-poisoning occurs without an attacker: a substantial fraction of an agent's speculations can resurface as factual claims at first rewrite across production memory systems. A defense evaluated only against adversarial injection will not detect this class.

4.4 Taint-margin escalation

Define \mu(e \to m) := |w(e \to m) - \theta|. If \mu < \delta for a pre-registered bandwidth \delta, flag the entry for human review before it can justify a sensitive action. This does not fix the gameability of \theta; it converts probing into an observable signal. Report the taint-margin distribution as a monitoring metric, and treat a spike in near-threshold edges as evidence of model drift or adversarial probing. This is an engineering defense, not a proved guarantee.

4.5 Adaptive attack portfolio with per-class ceilings

Evaluate against a pre-registered portfolio and report attack success rate per class, never as a single aggregate. The verified anchors are:

· Trojan Hippo (dormant payload via a single untrusted tool call): 85–100\% ASR against safety-aligned frontier models, with activation after long benign dwell (arXiv:2605.01970).
· Sleeper memory poisoning: storage success up to 99.8\% on GPT-5.5, retrieval rates 94–98\% on goal-adjacent evaluations, and conditional adversarial usage 60–89\% (arXiv:2605.15338).
· eTAMP (environment-injected trajectory poisoning): up to 32.5\% on GPT-5-mini, 23.4\% on GPT-5.2, 19.5\% on GPT-OSS-120B (arXiv:2604.02623). This is a stress-condition peak, not a baseline rate; report the clean-environment baseline alongside it.
· Query-only injection: high injection success and substantial downstream success through ordinary interaction.
· Self-consolidation laundering: to be measured deterministically in a harness; zero ceiling for sensitive-action justification.
· Paraphrase laundering: to be measured; tests DAG completeness.

Each ceiling \mathrm{ASR}_{\max}(a_k) must be justified by the threat model and the severity of the harm. A zero-ASR result in a deterministic harness is an existence result under stated conditions, not a transferable prior for the deployment distribution.

