5. Evaluation: redesigned Test B and corrected power

5.1 The plausibility confound and the three-arm design

The null H_0: \Lambda_0^{(\text{correlated})} = 1 is not the right comparison, because plausible false claims are both more likely to be emitted and more likely to be endorsed by any verifier. This plausibility confound inflates \Lambda_0 regardless of family. Test B must therefore compare three arms:

1. a same-family verifier,
2. a cross-family verifier,
3. a human verifier on the same stratified gold set.

The human arm estimates the plausibility-only baseline. The quantity of interest is the difference in \Lambda_0 between the same-family and human arms, with the cross-family arm bracketing it. Stratify by independent plausibility ratings, and pre-register the stratification.

5.2 Power on the conditional cell

The estimator \hat\Lambda_0 = \hat P(S=1 \mid A=1, Y=0)/\hat P(S=1 \mid Y=0) conditions on the cell (Y=0, A=1). Its variance is dominated by the numerator cell. With n_{Y0,A1} = 100, power to detect \Lambda_0 = 1.5 against the null is approximately 0.55–0.65, not 0.80. Reaching 0.80 requires n_{Y0,A1} of roughly 160–200, which may require oversampling the (Y=0, A=1) cell rather than increasing total n. Pre-register n_{Y0,S1} and the minimum detectable \Lambda_0 together; a gold set that conditions on the rare cell without a power analysis will produce an inconclusive result indistinguishable from a null.

5.3 The judge as a diagnostic, not a per-claim certificate

Three exchangeability violations block the certification interpretation: the judged sample is the producer's emitted claims, not a random sample; the claim distribution drifts; and an adaptive attacker can shift the distribution toward low-TPR cases. The judge may rank verifiers and monitor drift. It may not certify that a specific claim is SUPPORTED. Per-claim status remains the output of the external evidence pipeline.

5.4 Sensitivity–specificity correction, stratified

The correction of the verifier's positive rate for known error rates is the Rogan–Gladen sensitivity–specificity form:

\hat\phi_V^{(f)} = \frac{\hat p^{(f)} - \widehat{\mathrm{FPR}}^{(f)}}{\widehat{\mathrm{TPR}}^{(f)} - \widehat{\mathrm{FPR}}^{(f)}},

identifiable iff \mathrm{TPR}^{(f)} > \mathrm{FPR}^{(f)}. This estimates \phi_V within task family f; it is not an epidemiological prevalence correction and should not be described as one. Apply it within families, because a global calibration does not transfer to the difficult strata that dominate \phi_{\mathrm{sel}}. Report bootstrap or Clopper–Pearson intervals that propagate calibration uncertainty.

5.5 Unified gate

Version progression is gated by

\mathrm{UCB}_{1-\alpha}\big(\phi_{\mathrm{sel}}(V)\big) < \tau_{\text{task}},

with \tau_{\text{task}} fixed before the run. "Zero tolerance" is operationalized as a very small \tau_{\text{task}} for safety-critical categories, not as zero observed violations. The gate compares an upper confidence bound to a pre-registered threshold.

