6. Architecture and state-bound authorization

6.1 Subsystem decomposition

\mathcal{A} = \langle M,\ \mathcal{E},\ \mathcal{V},\ \mathcal{C},\ L,\ O,\ \mathcal{W} \rangle

with M the untrusted producer, \mathcal{E} evidence acquisition, \mathcal{V} verification, \mathcal{C} the policy controller, L the append-only ledger, O the independent observation channel, and \mathcal{W} the witness set. Every transition is a deterministic predicate over state and evidence; no transition is guarded by an LLM judgment. A model-produced authorization object is a proposal with no authority until the controller signs a corresponding grant.

6.2 State-bound grants

The evaluation–execution gap is the interval in which an approval valid at issuance is no longer valid at dispatch because evidence, policy, or authority has changed. The grant must bind the decision-relevant state:

\texttt{AuthorizationGrant} = \{\texttt{action\_id},\ \texttt{target\_tool},\ \texttt{arguments\_hash},\ \texttt{state\_basis\_hash},\ \texttt{policy\_version},\ \texttt{decision},\ \texttt{expiry},\ \texttt{controller\_signature}\}.

At EXECUTING, the controller recomputes state_basis_hash against the current logged state and checks policy_version. A mismatch invalidates the grant and returns the action to AWAITING_AUTH. The deterministic guards are the correct location for this recheck; it requires no model invocation.

6.3 Pre-registration fields

Every run is preceded by a signed pre-registration containing: task family; \tau_{\text{task}}; \Lambda_{\max}; the n_{\mathrm{eff}}^{\lambda} computation method with item-bootstrap interval; n_{\mathrm{eff,min}}; \theta and a sensitivity grid; \delta_{\text{margin}}; per-path q_p targets; per-class \mathrm{ASR}_{\max}; Test B power (mde_{\Lambda_0}, n_{Y0,A1}, n_{Y0,S1}, \alpha, power, FDR procedure); \delta_{\text{drift}}; damage window; the witness liveness contract (timeout, retry, failover, refusal semantics, minimum responding non-producer witnesses); the bypass-probe protocol; and the structural-completeness claim with its evidence class. The n_{Y0,S1} field is mandatory.

6.4 Observation-channel qualification

A hardware-attested runtime specification is a candidate for O, not an integration. Before adoption, a compatibility audit must establish: coverage of every tool path; independent verifiability without trusting the agent or producer; identity binding to the same action identifier as the checkpoint; and latency fit within T_c. If attestation is asynchronous, the interval in which L exists but O has not observed it is the damage window that determines T_c. The latency distribution must be measured, not assumed.

