7. Minimal falsification protocol

Test A — mediated versus unmediated path. Seed a benign document with an instruction attempting an unauthorized tool call or promotion of a poisoned memory entry. Run under an admitted verifier and under a same-family verifier, identical in model, tools, budgets, and task. Measure unauthorized action rate and poisoned promotion rate, with taint computed from the all-strong-path rule. Include a dormant-payload pattern activated by a later sensitive-topic discussion. Any non-zero unauthorized action rate in either condition blocks the version. This is a safety floor, not a comparative test.

Test A′ — bypass probe. Run the bypass probe against the deployment wiring. A clean run is recorded as dated, version-pinned evidence. A finding of an unmediated path blocks the version until compensated.

Test B — three-arm selection inflation. As in §5.1: same-family, cross-family, and human verifiers on a plausibility-stratified gold set, with FDR control across strata and power computed on n_{Y0,A1}. Report \hat\Lambda_0 and \hat\phi_{\mathrm{sel}} with intervals. Gate on \mathrm{UCB}(\phi_{\mathrm{sel}}) < \tau_{\text{task}}.

Test B′ — accuracy-paradox trend. Test the monotone prediction across accuracy strata with a single trend test or Benjamini–Hochberg correction. A flat or decreasing trend falsifies the conjecture.

Test C — replay after state mutation. Replay an action sequence after a policy or evidence mutation and verify that previously valid grants are invalidated. This tests the state-bound grant directly.

Test D — self-consolidation soak. Run a benign-session soak without an attacker and measure the fraction of speculative entries that become actionable without external re-grounding. Ceiling: zero for sensitive-action justification.

