9. Closing statement

The framework's mathematical content remains deliberately small: one identity, one spectral bound, one corrected detection bound and its weakest-path extension, one separation, one forking-refusal condition, one all-strong-path persistence proposition, and one completeness-inheritance argument. Everything else is a cited empirical premise with a stated scope, an explicitly labelled conjecture, or an engineering assumption marked as such.

The design commitments follow from the corrections rather than from intuition. Verifier admission is measured, because family labels predict correlation weakly and non-portably. Completeness is structural, because the statistical budget at agent horizons is five to a hundred effects, not thousands. Taint persistence requires all-strong paths, because a single strong edge does not survive a weak successor. Memory authority is bound at write time, because lineage is malleable and self-poisoning needs no attacker. Grants are state-bound, because approval at issuance is not authorization at dispatch. And the judge is a diagnostic, never a per-claim certificate, because the judged sample is selected and the adversary moves the distribution.

A theorem that is true but modest is worth more than a theorem that is impressive but conditional on an assumption the system violates. The contribution is not a stronger claim about the agent. It is a sharper account of which quantities must be measured, on which channel, with which failure mode in mind, before any claim about the agent can be made at all.

Appendices — Status-Classified

Scope statement. No component of this framework has been implemented, executed, trained, or evaluated. No harness has been built, no gold set has been assembled, no bypass probe has been run, and no measurement of \Lambda_0, q_p, n_{\mathrm{eff}}, or any attack success rate has been performed by the authors. Every empirical premise below is cited from external literature and carries that literature's scope; every numerical value is either a closed-form calculation under stated assumed parameters or a derived consequence of the framework's definitions. Where a quantity is unknown, it is marked open and a measurement protocol is specified rather than an estimate supplied.

Status taxonomy. Each result is labelled with one of the following.

· [PROVED] — exact, conditional only on the definitions given.
· [CALCULATED] — derived arithmetic under explicitly assumed parameters; not measured.
· [CITED] — empirical premise from external work, bounded to that work's datasets and conditions.
· [CONJECTURE] — unproved, with a stated falsifiable prediction.
· [ASSUMPTION] — engineering assumption, testable in principle, untested here.
· [DESIGN] — artifact specification (schema, invariant, protocol); not a result.
· [PROTOCOL] — a pre-registered test that has not been run.
· [OPEN] — no measurement exists; the measurement is required before any claim depending on it can be made.

