Appendix A. Selection Inflation: Proofs, Calculations, and the Conjecture

A.1 The identity — [PROVED]

Fix (\Omega, \mathcal{F}, P) and claims C \in \mathcal{C} with Y, S, A \in \{0,1\}. Assume P(Y=0) > 0 and P(S=1, Y=0) > 0, so \phi_{\mathrm{sel}} is defined. On the stratum Y=0, \{S=1\} and \{S=0\} partition the space:

\phi_V = \phi_{\mathrm{sel}}\,\pi_0 + P(A=1 \mid S=0, Y=0)\,(1-\pi_0).

Subtracting \phi_{\mathrm{sel}}\pi_0 and rearranging,

\phi_{\mathrm{sel}} - \phi_V = (1-\pi_0)\,\Delta,
\qquad
\Delta := P(A=1 \mid S=1, Y=0) - P(A=1 \mid S=0, Y=0).

Bayes on the same stratum gives P(S=1 \mid A=1, Y=0) = \phi_{\mathrm{sel}}\pi_0/\phi_V, hence

\Lambda_0 := \frac{P(S=1 \mid A=1, Y=0)}{P(S=1 \mid Y=0)} = \frac{\phi_{\mathrm{sel}}}{\phi_V},
\qquad
\phi_{\mathrm{sel}} = \Lambda_0\phi_V.

Both forms hold for arbitrarily dependent S and A. No distributional assumption is used.

A.2 Equivalence chain — [PROVED]

\phi_{\mathrm{sel}} = \phi_V \iff \Delta = 0 \iff \Lambda_0 = 1 \iff S \perp A \mid Y=0. The implications follow from A.1 with 1-\pi_0 > 0 and \phi_V > 0 on the stratum. The design consequence is a statement about measurement, not a result: admission must measure \Lambda_0, because raw accuracy does not identify it.

A.3 Variance of \hat\Lambda_0 — [CALCULATED]

To first order, with n_{Y0,A1,S1} the critical cell,

\mathrm{Var}(\hat\Lambda_0) \approx \Lambda_0^2\left(
\frac{1}{n_{Y0,A1,S1}} - \frac{1}{n_{Y0,A1}} + \frac{1}{n_{Y0,S1}} - \frac{1}{n_{Y0}}
\right),

with leading term 1/n_{Y0,A1,S1}. This is a delta-method expression under the stated ratio estimator; it is not an empirical finding.

A.4 Power for the worked example — [CALCULATED under assumed parameters]

Assume, for illustration only, n=2000, \pi_0=0.15, \phi_V=0.05, so n_{Y0} \approx 1700 and n_{Y0,A1} \approx 85. The standard error of \hat P(S=1 \mid A=1, Y=0) is approximately \sqrt{0.05\cdot 0.95/85} \approx 0.024, and the relative standard error of \hat\Lambda_0 is dominated by this term. Power to detect \Lambda_0 = 1.5 against the null at \alpha = 0.05 is then approximately 0.55–0.65; reaching 0.80 requires n_{Y0,A1} \approx 160–200. These are calculations, not measurements. The design rule they support is procedural: pre-register mde_lambda0 and n_Y0_A1 together, and if the gold set cannot achieve the required cell count, report the study as inconclusive by design rather than as a null.

A.5 Distribution dependence — [PROVED conditional on a stated premise] + [OPEN]

\Lambda_0 is a functional of (S,A) given Y=0. If that conditional differs between calibration and deployment, \Lambda_0 differs in general and the calibration does not transfer. The empirical premise that motivates a drift trigger is cited, not measured here: effective independence in judge panels varies by item class and difficulty [Kohli, arXiv:2605.29800]. The magnitude of drift on any deployment distribution is open; the trigger \delta_{\text{drift}} must be set and measured per deployment.

A.6 The accuracy paradox — [CONJECTURE]

If \pi_0 is decreasing in producer accuracy and \Delta is non-decreasing in producer accuracy, then \phi_{\mathrm{sel}} is increasing in producer accuracy. Differentiating the additive form with respect to an accuracy parameter \alpha,

\frac{d\phi_{\mathrm{sel}}}{d\alpha}
= \frac{d\phi_V}{d\alpha}
+ \frac{d\Delta}{d\alpha}(1-\pi_0)
- \Delta\frac{d\pi_0}{d\alpha},

the last term is positive when d\pi_0/d\alpha < 0, and the middle term is non-negative when d\Delta/d\alpha \ge 0. The conjecture is false if the second premise fails. Falsifiable prediction: \hat\Lambda_0 increases across accuracy strata under a single monotone trend test, or under Benjamini–Hochberg control if multiple strata are tested. No test has been run. Failure to control the false-discovery rate across many strata at \alpha = 0.05 would produce a spurious increasing trend at rate approaching one; that is a design constraint, not a result.

