Appendix B. Effective Sample Size: Bound, Interval, Baseline

B.1 Kish under equicorrelation — [PROVED conditional on equicorrelation]

If \rho_{ij} = \rho for all i \neq j, the variance of the average error indicator is inflated by 1+(K-1)\rho, and

n_{\mathrm{eff}}^{\mathrm{Kish}} = \frac{K}{1+(K-1)\bar\rho}

is exact. It is an approximation otherwise.

B.2 The eigenvalue bound — [PROVED]

Let R be the error-correlation matrix with eigenvalues \lambda_1 \ge \cdots \ge \lambda_K. Define n_{\mathrm{eff}}^{\lambda} := K/\lambda_{\max}(R). Since R is positive semidefinite with unit diagonal, \lambda_{\max} \ge 1, with equality iff R = I, so n_{\mathrm{eff}}^{\lambda} \le K. Since \mathbf{1}^{\top} R \mathbf{1} = K(1+(K-1)\bar\rho) and \lambda_{\max} \ge \mathbf{1}^{\top} R \mathbf{1}/K = 1+(K-1)\bar\rho,

n_{\mathrm{eff}}^{\lambda} = \frac{K}{\lambda_{\max}}
\le \frac{K}{1+(K-1)\bar\rho}
= n_{\mathrm{eff}}^{\mathrm{Kish}}.

Hence \min\{\text{Kish},\text{eigenvalue}\} = \text{eigenvalue}; the eigenvalue bound is used alone. The coincidence of the two on Kohli's panel is a numerical fact about that panel, not a general license.

B.3 Item clustering — [PROVED conditional on the additive model] + [OPEN]

Under the additive error model X_{ij} = U_i + V_{ij} with item-level shared variance \sigma_U^2 and judge-specific variance \sigma_V^2, the correlation matrix is equicorrelated with \rho = \sigma_U^2/(\sigma_U^2+\sigma_V^2), so Kish is exact under that model. The prior version's extra factor 1/(1+\sigma_V^2/\sigma_U^2) drove n_{\mathrm{eff}} to zero as \rho \to 0 and is not retained. The correct conservative quantity is the eigenvalue bound: when a single low-dimensional factor drives shared error, \lambda_{\max} \to K and n_{\mathrm{eff}}^{\lambda} \to 1, correctly reflecting that the panel supplies one effective vote on the items that matter. The magnitude of item clustering on any deployment panel is open.

B.4 Interval and baseline — [DESIGN] + [CITED]

Report n_{\mathrm{eff}}^{\lambda} with an item-level bootstrap interval: resample items with replacement, recompute the error indicators and correlation matrix on each resample, report the percentile interval. Judge-level resampling is invalid for this purpose because the correlation structure is a property of the item population. The point estimate without the interval is not an admission input; use the lower confidence bound. For scale, external work reports human-referenced effective sizes \nu_H = 4.24–6.50 and distributional-error effective sizes \nu_{\mathrm{MSE}} = 2.30–3.75 for 32-judge panels against ChaosNLI human distributions [arXiv:2609.21277], and approximately two effective votes for a nine-judge, seven-family panel [Kohli, arXiv:2605.29800]. These are cited anchors, not measurements of this framework's deployment panel.

B.5 Admission condition — [DESIGN]

Evaluate admission against n_{\mathrm{eff}}^{\mathrm{cons}} := n_{\mathrm{eff}}^{\lambda} at its item-bootstrap lower confidence bound, and require n_{\mathrm{eff}}^{\mathrm{cons}} \ge n_{\min}(\text{task}). The threshold n_{\min} is a pre-registered design parameter, not a measured quantity.

B.6 Caution on a frequently cited anchor — [CITED with explicit non-transfer]

The result that Kish gave 1.66 while measured effective votes were 1.00 comes from a single program-based experiment using partial test oracles, not LLM judges [dev.to report]. It is a warning that Kish can overstate independence; it is not a transferable correction factor and is not used as one here. The deployment estimate is n_{\mathrm{eff}}^{\lambda} on the deployment panel with item bootstrap, which is open.

