Appendix C. Structural Completeness: Corrected Bound, All-Strong Paths, Forking

C.1 The corrected detection bound — [PROVED] + [CALCULATED table]

Under independent observation with probability q per effect, P(\text{at least one undetected omission}) = 1-q^N. Setting this below \epsilon and solving with \ln q < 0,

1 - q^N \le \epsilon
\iff q^N \ge 1-\epsilon
\iff N \le \frac{\ln(1-\epsilon)}{\ln q}.

The prior form N \le \ln\epsilon/\ln q is not a small-\epsilon approximation; \ln(1-\epsilon) \approx -\epsilon, whereas \ln\epsilon is negative and larger in magnitude. The table below is a [CALCULATED] consequence of the corrected formula at \epsilon = 0.05; it is not a measurement.

q N_{\max} (corrected) prior (incorrect)
0.99 5 298
0.995 10 598
0.999 51 2994
0.9995 102 5990

Holding N = 3000 below 5\% would require q \ge 0.99998 on every effect. The bound also describes the probability that a blind spot exists, not that an omission is detected; those are different events, and the latter is what completeness requires.

C.2 The structural guarantee — [ASSUMPTION] + [PROTOCOL]

Completeness assurance is structural, not statistical.

S1 (mediation). Every effect-producing action reaches a mediated channel. No unmediated path exists from the agent process to a governed target.

S2 (bypass absence). The absence of unmediated paths is tested by an enumerating bypass probe; a clean run is recorded as dated, version-pinned evidence.

S1 is an engineering property of a deployment, not a theorem; S2 is evidence, not proof. A structural finding of this kind is true of the framework for every adopter and cannot be configured away; a clean probe run is evidence that a particular wiring has no bypass at a particular time. The statistical q characterizes only the residual: effects that traverse a mediated channel but are not captured by O. The residual bound is

N \le \frac{\ln(1-\epsilon)}{\ln q_{\mathrm{sys}}},
\qquad
q_{\mathrm{sys}} := \min_{p \in \mathcal{P}} q_p.

No system-level guarantee can exceed the guarantee on the weakest path. q_p on any deployment has not been measured. A protocol for measuring it is specified in E.3; the values are open.

C.3 All-strong-path persistence — [PROVED]

Let \mathbb{T} = \{\bot, \mathsf{T}, \mathsf{U}, \mathsf{X}, \mathsf{P}\} with \bot < \mathsf{T} < \mathsf{U} < \mathsf{X} < \mathsf{P}, join \vee = \max, and for edge weight w and threshold \theta,

w \otimes t :=
\begin{cases}
t, & w \ge \theta,\\
\bot, & w < \theta.
\end{cases}

Proposition. If every path from an ancestor e_0 with t(e_0) \ge \mathsf{X} to m consists entirely of edges of weight \ge \theta, then t(m) \ge \mathsf{X}.

Proof. Let G_\theta contain exactly the edges of weight \ge \theta. The hypothesis says e_0 and m lie in the same connected component of G_\theta. Induct on the length of a path in G_\theta from e_0 to m. Base: length zero, m = e_0, t(m) = t(e_0) \ge \mathsf{X}. Step: let e be the penultimate node on a strong path, so e \to m has weight \ge \theta. By the induction hypothesis t(e) \ge \mathsf{X}. Then w(e \to m) \otimes t(e) = t(e) \ge \mathsf{X}, and the join at m is at least that value. Hence t(m) \ge \mathsf{X}. \square

The one-strong-edge condition is false by the counterexample e_0 \xrightarrow{0.9} a \xrightarrow{0.1} m with \theta = 0.5: the edge e_0 \to a is strong, but a \to m is weak and contributes \bot, so t(m) = \bot. The operational rule is to compute G_\theta and require every external ancestor of a sensitive-action justification to lie in the same strong component as the justification. The prior induction that selected the last strong edge was ill-ordered; propagation is forward from the ancestor.

C.4 Forking and refusal semantics — [PROVED for the negative claim] + [ASSUMPTION for the deployment contract]

A Merkle consistency proof shows only that a presented branch extends some previously accepted state; it does not show that the producer holds no other branch. Detecting forking requires a witness that retains the last checkpoint accepted for a given producer-and-log identity and refuses a presented predecessor inconsistent with that retained state. Registration with a transparency service without per-log predecessor state proves inclusion, not non-equivocation.

Liveness assumption L1 — [ASSUMPTION]. Within each interval of length T_c, at least one non-producer witness is online and responsive, retains its last accepted checkpoint outside the producer's administrative domain, and refuses inconsistent predecessors. The witness set is chosen so that no single administrative domain controls a majority. If L1 fails for longer than the damage window, forking is undetectable in principle and the residual bound does not apply to effects in that interval. T_c \le \texttt{damage\_window} is a design constraint, not a measured property.

C.5 Receiver-signed receipts and context-as-parent — [DESIGN]

The tool receipt is signed by the receiver (the tool gateway), not only by the agent, and binds to the same action identifier as the checkpoint. observer_ref records which independent channel also observed the effect, making the injective map of completeness checkable. The retrieval context at generation time is a derivation parent: if m is produced while a poisoned context c is active, c \in \mathrm{anc}(m) even if the agent does not quote it. Omitting context-as-parent is a declassification channel of the same kind as an unrecorded paraphrase. These are specifications; whether any given deployment records them is open until a harness enforces the invariants of F.4.

