Appendix D. Memory Governance: Origin, Modality, Portfolio

D.1 Write-time origin binding — [ASSUMPTION supported by cited analysis]

The derivation DAG is not a stable ground truth: lineage is malleable through summarization, trusted-tool echo, and manufactured corroboration. Taint is to be assigned at first write by the ingesting component and immutably bound to the entry; later derivations may not lower it; an edge that would carry a lower taint than its parent is rejected. This makes propagation monotone by construction rather than by threshold tuning. The supporting analysis is cited, not performed here: a machine-checked separation theorem for content- and lineage-based defenses and a write-time origin-binding construction are reported in external work on securing long-term agent memory [cited]. No implementation of that construction exists in this framework. Its effectiveness is open for this framework's deployment.

D.2 Modality and self-poisoning — [ASSUMPTION] + [OPEN]

The lattice governs origin, not modality. A speculation promoted to a factual claim should require new external evidence, not merely a recorded derivation edge. Self-poisoning without an attacker is a distinct failure class; its rate in this framework's harness is open, and the protocol in D.5 is specified to measure it. No value is asserted here.

D.3 Taint-margin escalation — [DESIGN] + [OPEN]

Define \mu(e \to m) := |w(e \to m) - \theta|. If \mu < \delta for a pre-registered \delta, flag the entry for human review before it can justify a sensitive action. This does not fix the gameability of \theta; it converts probing into an observable signal. The distribution of \mu and the effectiveness of the escalation are open and are monitored, not assumed.

D.4 Attack anchors — [CITED with explicit scope]

The adaptive portfolio reports per-class attack success rate, never a single aggregate. The following are cited values from external work, not measurements of this framework.

· Trojan Hippo (dormant payload via a single untrusted tool call): 85–100\% ASR against frontier models from OpenAI and Google, with activation after 100 benign sessions; memory-system defenses reduce ASR to 0–5\% at utility cost [arXiv:2605.01970].
· Sleeper memory poisoning: storage success up to 99.8\%; conditional adversarial usage 60–89\% among successful retrievals [arXiv:2605.15338].
· eTAMP (environment-injected trajectory poisoning): ASR up to 32.5\% on GPT-5-mini, 23.4\% on GPT-5.2, 19.5\% on GPT-OSS-120B. This is a stress-condition peak; Frustration Exploitation increases ASR up to eightfold, and the clean-environment baseline must be reported alongside it [arXiv:2604.02623].
· Query-only injection: high injection success and substantial downstream success through ordinary interaction.
· Self-consolidation laundering: to be measured deterministically in a harness; zero ceiling for sensitive-action justification. Rate open.
· Paraphrase laundering: to be measured; tests DAG completeness. Rate open.

Each ceiling \mathrm{ASR}_{\max}(a_k) is a design parameter justified by the threat model and harm severity. A zero-ASR result in a deterministic harness is an existence result under stated conditions, not a transferable prior for the deployment distribution.

D.5 Protocol for the unmeasured classes — [PROTOCOL]

Self-consolidation: run a benign-session soak with no attacker, measure the fraction of speculative entries that become actionable without external re-grounding, ceiling zero for sensitive-action justification. Paraphrase laundering: construct derivations through the agent's own summarizer and through trusted-tool echo, measure whether taint survives, ceiling zero for sensitive justification. Neither has been run.

