The n_Y0_A1 and n_Y0_S1 fields are both mandatory: the numerator of \hat\Lambda_0 conditions on A=1 and the denominator on S=1, and pre-registering only total n leaves both critical cells implicit.

E.2 Illustrative power — [CALCULATED under assumed parameters]

The calculation in A.4 uses assumed parameters and is not a measurement. Its purpose is procedural: it shows that a gold set with \phi_V=0.05 and n_{Y0}\approx 1700 produces roughly 85 critical-cell observations, sufficient for marginal power to detect \Lambda_0=1.5 and insufficient for 0.80. A real study must supply its own assumed parameters and its own power calculation before running.

E.3 Per-path coverage — [PROTOCOL] + [OPEN]

For each tool path p, measure \hat q_p with a Wilson or Clopper–Pearson interval, and compute \hat q_{\mathrm{sys}} := \min_p \mathrm{LCB}_{1-\alpha}(\hat q_p). The residual budget is N \le \ln(1-\epsilon)/\ln\hat q_{\mathrm{sys}}. Structural S1–S2 is the primary assurance; \hat q_{\mathrm{sys}} covers the residual. No path coverage has been measured.

E.4 Per-class ceilings — [DESIGN]

Ceilings are derived from the threat model and harm severity. For irreversible harm, the exploratory ceiling is zero; for detectable and recoverable harm, a small positive ceiling may be acceptable. Per-class reporting is required because an aggregate ASR can mask a low-frequency, high-severity class.

