H.7 Taint lattice and the corrected all-strong-path condition — [PROVED]

```
   Lattice T:   ⊥ < T < U < X < P,   join ∨ = max,  meet ∧ = min.

        P  (known-poisoned)
        │
        X  (external / untrusted)
        │
        U  (unverified)
        │
        T  (trusted internal derivation)
        │
        ⊥  (no information)

   Propagation:  w ⊗ t = t if w ≥ θ, else ⊥.
   At m:  t(m) = ⋁_{e ∈ anc(m)} ( w(e→m) ⊗ t(e) ).

   Counterexample to the one-strong-edge condition (θ = 0.5):

        e_0  ──0.9──►  a  ──0.1──►  m
        t= X           t= ?          t= ?

        Edge e_0 → a is strong (0.9 ≥ 0.5):  carries X to a.
        Edge a → m is weak  (0.1 < 0.5):      contributes ⊥.
        Join at m = ⊥.   ⇒   t(m) = ⊥  ≱ X.
        One strong edge per path is NOT sufficient.

   Correct condition: every path from e_0 to m is entirely strong.

        e_0  ──0.9──►  a  ──0.8──►  m
        t= X           t= X          t= X     (all edges ≥ θ)

        Then e_0 and m lie in the same strong-edge component G_θ,
        and t(m) ≥ X by forward induction.                    [PROVED, C.3]
```

